From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Goyvaerts <jonathan.goyvaerts@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 19:05:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: aop: Add T6040 support

The T6040 (M4 Pro) AOP firmware takes the same boot arguments as on the
T6020 (EC0p 0x100_0000_0000, nCal 0, alig 64, AOPt 0) and starts through
RTKit over one mailbox, but two things differ:

- Linux resets the AOP's DART, and no mapping the bootloader made
  survives that. The firmware fetches its external code segment
  (__ETEXT) at the EC0p IOVA and writes its log segment (__OS_LOG) at
  its physical address; without the latter it stops within a second on
  a storm of DART faults. The bootloader reserves both segments as
  /reserved-memory/aop-etext@<phys> and aop-oslog@<phys>. Map them into
  the AOP's DMA domain before the coprocessor starts: each node must be
  unique, page aligned and at most 1 MiB, each mapping is read back, and
  nothing is started if any check fails. The mappings are removed on
  unbind once the coprocessor is quiesced, and kept, like the other
  buffers, when the shutdown cannot be confirmed.
- The firmware is shut down on unbind. When its start fails after the
  CPU was released, the driver tears down as usual and then clears the
  CPU run bit again, leaving the AOP halted as the bootloader left it.

Signed-off-by: Jonathan Goyvaerts <jonathan.goyvaerts@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 drivers/soc/apple/aop.rs | 232 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 222 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

